# What each rule actually examined, and whether that is enough to mean anything. One row per
# rule: how many items it looked at, how many breached it, and a verdict on the count itself.
#
# A rule that examined nothing did not pass
#   This repository names the trap in one sentence, "a bound with nothing to bound passes
#   loudest", and this is where it is answered rather than restated. An empty violations table
#   beside a zero here is a checker reporting on its own silence.
#     examined = 0  `VACUOUS`. The rule proves nothing whatever.
#     examined < 3  `thin`. One or two rows is a demonstration, not evidence.
#     otherwise     `ok`.
#
# A thin verdict is about authorship
#   It is usually a fact about authorship, not about the world. If a rule examines one row, the
#   question is who wrote the corpus and what they filed, not whether the model is wrong. A
#   fixture can raise the number honestly, because a fixture is a stipulation; a corpus filing
#   cannot, because that would mean inventing an observation nobody made.
#
# Why this groups over a roster join
#   The vacuous row is the whole reason this is a `GROUP BY` over a roster join rather than over a
#   population. Group a filtered population and a rule with nothing to examine produces no row at
#   all, so the checker's most important verdict would be delivered by silence, which is
#   indistinguishable from the rule not existing. Every check emits its roster row first, so
#   `examined = 0` is a row you can read.
#
# One relation, no restated population
#   Every count here is over the same relation the violations are filtered from. Nothing restates
#   a population, so nothing here can be about a different set of rows than the rule it reports
#   on.
-- folds/rule_subjects.sqlc, one row per rule either side names.
SELECT f.subject                        AS rule,
       f.answered                       AS examined,
       f.violated                       AS violations,
       CASE WHEN f.answered = 0 THEN '⛔ VACUOUS - proves nothing'
            WHEN f.answered < 3 THEN 'thin'
            ELSE 'ok' END               AS verdict
FROM (
    :compose(folds/rule_subjects.sqlc)
) f
ORDER BY examined DESC, rule
